## Main results

* **Part 13 (odd blocks).** The abstract Brezzi a priori bound and its perturbation versions
  (`brezzi_bound`, `perturbed_apriori`); the constant chains of Lemma W at block ratio 3
  (`lemmaW3_chain`), of the curvature transfer CT′ at ratio 3 (`ct3_chain`, `ct3_apriori`), of
  Theorem U at ratio 3, a one-shot loss bound uniform in the scale and the volume (`thmU3_bound`),
  and of the tree-gauge loss at ratio 3 (`tb3_bound`); and the finite counts of the side-3 centre
  comb, checked by `decide` (`bg3_assembly`, `bg3_mean`). The lattice inputs enter only as
  hypotheses.
* **Part 14 (half-pinned tensorisation).** A stable decomposition is equivalent to a Poincaré
  inequality, and the minimal splitting attains the constant (`lions`, `minimal_splitting`); the
  half-pinned decomposition, with one large unpinned block and local boxes, gives the half-pinned
  inequality with an explicit constant (`halfPinned_stable`, `halfPinnedVT`, `halfPinnedConst_le`),
  with the extension constant below 3 (`extension_const_lt_three`); the localisation identity and
  bound that the inequality feeds (`hloc_identity`, `hloc_bound`); decay of powers from a spectral
  gap (`neumann_decay`, `gap_decay`); and a transfer identity with a lift-energy bound used in four
  dimensions (`transfer_sum_zero`, `transfer_image`, `twoLevel4D_bound`, `liftEnergy_le`).
* **Part 15 (infinite-volume bridge).** Convergence of vectors between varying Hilbert spaces
  (`SConv`, `WConv`); Mosco-type convergence of closed subspaces gives strong convergence of the
  orthogonal projections (`starProjection_sconv`); uniform bounds pass to the limit
  (`tsum_le_of_tendsto`, `abs_inner_le_of_conv`); for a reversible Markov kernel on a finite space,
  a coupling contraction gives a Poincaré inequality and variance tensorisation with an explicit
  constant, sharp on a lazy two-state chain (`poincare_of_coupling`,
  `variance_tensorisation_of_coupling`); coercivity from a bounded lift (`coercive_of_lift`); and
  two-sided telescoping along a finitely supported time axis (`past_step`, `future_step`,
  `cut_step`).

Every part ends with examples on concrete data that apply its theorems; each example's docstring
says which hypotheses it lists and which of its instances are degenerate.

## Scope

These are results about proof methods at Gaussian order (Parts 13 and 14) and abstract statements
used by one proof architecture (Part 15); lattice, Gaussian and measure inputs enter only as
hypotheses. Nothing here is about the Yang–Mills measure. The estimates (D) (the cube estimate
